From: Adam Matthews <52178922+adamsthws@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 20:10:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] esp32c3 gpio: input pins idle high, as with pull-ups

Run on its own, the fork drives no input pins, so they all read low. The
S60's button (GPIO9) is active low, so Tasmota saw it held, and after 40
seconds reset itself to its defaults, losing its Wi-Fi. Inputs now idle high,
as with pull-ups. Offered upstream as lcgamboa/qemu #17.
---
 hw/gpio/esp32c3_gpio.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/gpio/esp32c3_gpio.c b/hw/gpio/esp32c3_gpio.c
index b9cf603..8bcd9fe 100644
--- a/hw/gpio/esp32c3_gpio.c
+++ b/hw/gpio/esp32c3_gpio.c
@@ -167,6 +167,11 @@ static void esp32c3_gpio_init(Object *obj)
     ESP32C3GPIOState *s = ESP32C3_GPIO(obj);
     /* Set the default value for the property */
     object_property_set_int(obj, "strap_mode", ESP32C3_STRAP_MODE_FLASH_BOOT, &error_fatal);
+    /* Autoflash emulator: nothing drives the pins when run standalone (PICSimLab
+     * normally does), so let inputs idle high, as with pull-ups. Otherwise an
+     * active-low button (Tasmota's Button1) reads as held, and holding it for
+     * 40 s resets Tasmota to its defaults. */
+    s->parent.gpio_in = 0x003FFFFF;   /* GPIO0..21 */
 
     memory_region_init_io(&s->parent.iomem, obj, &gpio_ops, s, TYPE_ESP32C3_GPIO,
                           0x1000);
